A||||11||Ozzy||Replacement Bushings...
Z||000000||Ozzy||08-19-99||11:19 PM||||Hey folks...<p>In preperation for repairs and improvements on my '88 coupe front end I have embarked upon my first Fiero parts ordering expedition.<p>A few thanks are in order...thanks to 'theogre' for leading me to this site;<p>http://www.suspension.com <p>This guy (the owner) was very helpfull and tolerant of my questions and sold me the full set of front Prothane urethane replacements for $47.00. This is $20.00 cheaper than a popular competitor. <p>I also lucked out and had a CarQuest salesman stop by our shop trying to sell us parts for our fleet of trucks. I told him we wouldn't be needing his services for that but had him price the Fiero LBJ's (Moog) and gas shocks and struts. $47.50 for the balljoints and $13.00 for a pair of replacement gas shocks for the front. I forget the name of the shocks but don't expect them to be great. $37.75 for replacement gas struts...Will see how the front shocks perform before installing these as I suspect the strut replacement is a pain!<br>I just felt guilty since he helped me out on the LBJ's. I had been quoted as much as $80.00 ea for Moog LBJ's elsewhere! (thnx Rod)<p>Ozzy
Z||000001||theogre||08-20-99||12:27 AM||||well first off, you're welcome..... &gt;<IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15> After such a shitty day it's nice to know someone gives a crap to say thanks.<p>can he do those joints in volume? there aren't to many people here that don't need them. Send me his phone #. I want to replace all mine soon. Mine has 136K on it. Moog Rocks. There's a very good reason they are NASCAR listed as approved parts.<p>the front shocks,,,,, if it's just a daily driver, then they should do. For hard stuff, find ut who made them first. Even then they might not do.<p>Your right, the rears are a pain. You likely need "cam bolts" for them. you also need to have someone change the springs, etc, or rent tools. With the hasle involved in the struts, I don't think I'd "go cheap" on them. not worth the trouble to redo them in 6 months because they suck. Shock jobs don't need an alignment,,,, struts do.
Z||000002||Ozzy||08-20-99||08:57 AM||||Hey ogre...No problem man! You are a large contributor here and have helped me a bunch with various things. Also the other dudes that pitch there knowledge and experience to this site...I have seen something of how the automotive repair trade works here and its very easy to get screwed. This helps eliminate that factor.<p>Anyway, the guys name at Suspension Restoration Parts Co. is Bruce. The phone no. is: 209 578 5101. He told me that he is now the largest purchaser of these busshings from Prothane. I'm sure he can provide quantity. He's a real cool dude and I told him I was gonna let everyone know on this forum. Tell me what you think if you deal with him.<p>Shocks are Mearmont. I've never heard of them before but...Yeah I wus thinking the same thing on the rear. As much as I want to become familiar with every aspect of this vehichle, I think I might pay someone to do those for me. Let me know if those shocks are a shitty brand or not If you would.<p>Thnx <p>Ozzy
Z||000003||ChadMan||08-20-99||11:53 AM||||I have KYB shocks/struts on my 88 Coupe and I love them.  I have 3 years and 33K miles on them so far and their performance hasn't diminished at all (yet).  The car rides nicely, yet handles great and stays planted even if you hit a big bump in the middle of a turn (unlike my 86 which launches sideways!).  The KYB's are pricey, though.
Z||000004||Ozzy||08-20-99||01:24 PM||||I lied...Shocks and struts are all Macpherson. shocks were $26.04 / pair.<br>Struts were $74.93 / pair. All are gas. Are these struts worth installing???<p>Ozzy
Z||000005||theogre||08-20-99||03:25 PM||||I ment the ball joint guy..... 
Z||000006||cmyokley||08-20-99||04:18 PM||||OZZY,<p>One word of cation, use the silcone grease supplied with the poly bushings and if you can get some extra use that too. The small tubes I got in my kit was not enough. The instructions don't say anything about using the grease for squeaking problems just for installation reasons BUT it is definitely needed for squeaking reasons. I am going to have to remove my front A-Arms to add grease to the outside faces of the bushings because I am getting squeaking coming from the bushings....and I do not want to do this. If anyone has an idea to eliminate the noise without disassembly please let me know.<p>The poly bushings are very worth the time it will take to install.  Have fun.
Z||000007||Ozzy||08-20-99||05:57 PM||||HEy ogre...Will find out on the ball joints . This dude ran these thru on a shops account to get me the discount. I thought u ment bushings.(brb)<p>cmyokley...gotchya on the grease. I've been told that the trick is to run a tap thru the inner hole of the bushing...just sized to leave some small grooves in the poly urethane to hold the grease.<p>Ozzy
Z||000008||lowCG||08-20-99||10:19 PM||||The grease you want for plastics(and urethane)is a Teflon grease,a couple of grooves(inside the bushing)work too for holding a pocket of grease to supply the bushing.
Z||000009||theogre||08-21-99||02:55 PM||||part 1 of a urathane upgrade.... clean clean clean. all surfaces the bushing will contact must be clean.<p>If you still get face squeeks. you can add a washer of brass, aluminum or certain plastics, between the face and the frame. (if you can find sheet teflon use it.)These materials are resistant to dirt and will provide clean, hard, flat, contact surfaces to the bushings and frame.The center hole should be big enough to clear the inner sleave so that the washer can float. Even this isn't always a permenant solution, but it will increase the service interval by a huge amount.<p>Another thing is to use a lubricant on the outside diameter that Will wash out. You don't want the OD to move in the shell at all after assembly, but it you don't use anything they are next to imposible to get in. 
Z||000010||lowCG||08-21-99||07:24 PM||||Yeah,liquid Dawn works good as an elastomeric assembly solution,on the outside of the bushing,dries up to a crusty haze after an hour or so.
Z||000011||Ozzy||08-22-99||03:00 AM||||Update!<p>Well, it's late and I've just finished up installing new LBJ's, urehtane bushings & front shocks. <p>LBJ's went pretty simple with the help of a mechanic friends shop press. But the bushings...ugh! I had absolutley no trouble installing the new ones but removing the old...Now ain't the a bag o' fun?!!! I had to burn those suckers out and it seemed to take forever but I can definatley say I have a dramatic improvement in the way the car handles and tracks now. I did not separate the steering linkages and reinstalled the upper control arm supports as near to the original position as I could manage. I will get a wheel alignment next week  but even as it is now the front feels tight and responsive. Glad I got it done so I can move on to the next thing.<p>Ozzy
